# qc-assert-no-full-env-dump.allowlist
# One entry per line: path:line | classification | YYYY-MM-DD reason | named remediation
# These four are RISK — the full pm2 jlist blob transits an unfiltered variable.
# Allowlisted with a dated reason per Rule 3.5 stage 1 (warn-mode); remediation is stage 2.
scripts/fleet-audit-remediate.sh:52 | RISK | 2026-07-27 Full pm2 jlist JSON lands in shell variable `jl`; never printed, but `set -x` or error trace would leak. | Capture through filter_pm2_record (loop_common.py:164-172) instead.
scripts/u126-remediate.sh:76 | RISK | 2026-07-27 Full pm2 jlist JSON lands in shell variable `pm2_json`; near-duplicate of fleet-audit-remediate.sh:52. | Capture through filter_pm2_record (loop_common.py:164-172) instead.
shared-utils/fleet_refresh_runner.py:619 | RISK | 2026-07-27 The ONLY site that indexes pm2_env by name (:624). Uses subprocess.run(["pm2","jlist"]) list form (invisible to string regex). | Read status through filter_pm2_record (loop_common.py:164-172); use pm2 describe/pm2 list instead.
update-skills.sh:6974 | RISK | 2026-07-27 (line re-derived 2026-08-03, was :5583, before that :5442 — drifts on every intervening edit) Fleet updater — full pm2 jlist JSON lands in shell variable `_ccp_list`; a hard fail here blocks every fleet roll (Rule 3.5). | Capture through a field filter like filter_pm2_record; use targeted pm2 describe that does not emit environments.
scripts/ghl-mcp-assert-runtime.sh:326 | filtered | 2026-08-03 The blob is NEVER captured: `pm2 jlist` is piped STRAIGHT into $_GHL_FILTER_PM2_RECORD, which emits only six non-secret fields (script path, stop_exit_codes, GHL_TOOL_PROFILE, PORT, MCP_SERVER_PORT, GHL_MCP_LOG_DIR) as KEY=VALUE lines. No temp file, no unfiltered variable, nothing to leak under `set -x`. This is the loop_common.py:164-172 filter_pm2_record pattern; the guard cannot classify a SHELL pipeline as 'filtered' (that branch is Python-AST-only), which is why this entry exists. | None — the guard's shell path should learn to recognise a same-line pipe into a filter, as its Python path already does.
